    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 280018\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n518 PM PDT Sun Sep 27 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* Increased chances for showers and thunderstorms arrive Monday \n  and Tuesday along with cooler temperatures.\n\n* Stronger storms across the Colorado River Valley and northwest\n  Arizona may produce flash flooding, small hail, and strong wind\n  gusts. \n\n* Mild weather and warming temperatures are expected during the\n  second half of the week.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...through next weekend.\n\nAfternoon satellite imagery depicts a deepening West Coast trough\ndigging southward into northern California. Meanwhile, remnant \nmid and high level moisture from Tropical Storm Odalys continue to\nstream northward into the Southwestern US, with a broad cloud \nshield and some light shower activity developing as far north as \nthe Las Vegas Valley. These two features will work together over\nthe coming 48 hours to produce widespread unsettled conditions,\nand pockets of heavy rainfall mainly south and east of the I-15 \ncorridor through Tuesday evening. In the meantime, light showers\nand localized gusty winds will remain possible through the evening\nacross southern Nevada associated with increasing moisture above \na dry sub-cloud layer. With DCAPE values of 1000-1500 across the \nregion, some gusts exceeding 40 mph will be possible, and the KLAS\nairport recently gusted to 45 mph as some weak showers moved \noverhead. \n\nMoisture will continue to stream northward overnight with light\nstratiform precipitation expanding in coverage across Northwest\nArizona and the southern reaches of the Colorado River Valley.\nThis precip shield will largely be associated with moisture\nstreaming northward from Tropical Storm Odalys, and will shift\neastward through the day on Monday. Meanwhile, an incoming trough\nwill approach from northern California through the afternoon\nresulting in an increasingly diffluent flow aloft along with\nsteepening lapse rates as the mid-levels cool. This will result \nin expanding instability across the Colorado River Valley and \nNorthwest Arizona through the afternoon hours. Todays high \nresolution guidance continues to struggle on how far west to \ninitiate convection, but most of the convective allowing models \nfavor areas near or just east of Las Vegas as the initial storm \ndevelopment focus area, with activity expanding into the river \nvalley and northwest AZ through the afternoon and evening hours. \nGiven the dynamic forcing involved with the incoming low, and \nstrong southwesterly wind shear, a few severe storms with hail to\nquarter size and gusty surface winds will be possible.\n\nTraining storms producing pockets of intense rainfall can also be\nexpected given the deep layer southwesterly shear present ahead \nof the incoming low. Given this, the presence of some transient \ntropical moisture, and agreement of CAM guidance - expanded the \nFlood Watch to include the Colorado River Valley and far southern \nClark County. A few weak showers and storms may also form closer \nto the low center across the Sierra and Great Basin, but are not\nexpected to be of significant impact. \n\nMoisture will wrap around the low as it moves into Arizona on\nTuesday with lingering showers expected across southern Nevada and\nnorthwest Arizona. By Wednesday, the low will shift east with low\namplitude high pressure building back in and a warming trend\ncommencing. \n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 00Z Forecast Package...Isolated \nshowers capable of producing gusts of 25 to 30 knots will continue \ninto the early evening before diminishing. Prevailing gusts of 15 to \n20 knots will subside after sunset, but southwesterly winds will \nremain elevated between 8 and 10kts overnight. CIGs will generally \nremain SCT-BKN AOA 15kft AGL, although CIGs down to 10kft AGL are \npossible with shower activity. A similar daily wind pattern is \nexpected again tomorrow, with speeds and gusts slightly lower than \ntoday. VCTS was maintained after 19Z Monday to account for \nincreasing confidence in convective development that may continue \ninto Monday night. Any storms will have the capability to produce \nstrong winds with erratic directions, heavy rain, lightning, and \nlower ceilings.\n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast \nCalifornia...For the 00Z Forecast Package...Typical daily wind \npatterns are expected at area terminals through the TAF period. \nGusty southerly winds will persist in most areas through sunset. The \nwestern Mojave Desert, including KDAG, will be the exception, with \nsouth to southwest winds continuing into the overnight period. Light \nshower activity is expected around the Las Vegas Valley and CRV \nterminals into the evening, with an area of additional rainfall \nimpacting KEED and KIFP late tonight and Monday morning. More \nwidespread convection is expected Monday afternoon and evening \nacross southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southern and eastern \nSan Bernardino counties. CIGs will generally remain SCT-BKN AOA \n15kft AGL, although CIGs down to 10kft AGL are possible with shower \nactivity.\n\n&&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Outler\nAVIATION...Moore/Planz\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
